well we're joined Now by The Shadow  leveling up secretary Lisa Nando thank  you for being with us and we've just  been listening to the Jacob re small  interview I was quite interested what he  said about the working week the 48 hour  week as some regulation we should look  at all different regulations  particularly those under EU law do you  agree with him I I didn't hear the  precise thing that he said about the  48-hour week but I certainly got the  general thrust that this is a government  that is going to try to deregulate in  order to fuel growth in the economy I  think the problem for them is that a lot  of these ideas have been tried before  we've already had Rishi sunak for  example trying to scrap or lower stamp  Duty during the pandemic it just pushed  up house prices it didn't help  first-time buyers in fact there was  quite a bit of evidence that it made  housing affordability worse I think the  government really does need to think  seriously about not just what help  they're going to provide but who pays  for that help that was the big  unanswered question from that interview  that you just did with the business  secretary everybody accepts that  businesses families need help this  winter the question is are we going to  tax the profits of people who've done  well in the last few years or are we  going to load more and more of that cost  onto struggling small businesses and  struggling families across the country  as you say it is an unanswered question  still we are asking it Jacob reesman  says look we've got to wait till Friday  which I guess is two days away  um to find out exactly how they do a  plan to pay from it and what else are  you looking forward for on Friday well  we've got we've got to have a plan to  help people now and we've got to make  sure that the burden falls on those who  are most able to Bear it so you know as  we put forward plans to raise the  digital sales tax for example so that  the big internet Giants pay a bit more  we think that would raise about two  billion pounds we could use half of that  to help energy intensive Industries who  are really struggling right now and the  other half as a fund to raise the  threshold for business rates relief so  that a lot of small businesses will be  taken out of business business rates  altogether and others would find that  they had help with other costs at a time  when Energy prices are soaring those are  the sort of proposals that we want to  see the government bringing forward not  more of this loading more of the costs  displacing them onto businesses and  families who are already struggling by  higher taxis for a very long period of  time we want to see fairness in the  system and that's what the government  has to answer I mean I guess what  they're trying to do with business isn't  it is to try and remove some of that tax  burden as well particularly in this the  uh the investment zones as well  potentially slashing taxes for business  and even potentially for workers as well  in deprived areas to try and boost  growth is that something you think would  be a good idea well this isn't a new  idea so this is an idea that's been  around for a very long time it was tried  under the last Labor government George  Osborne it was his big idea in 2011 and  we've still got a lot of these they're  called Enterprise zones at the moment  they're being renamed investment zones  the problem with what the government is  briefing is that these are not  particularly focused on deprived areas  and the areas that have them don't  necessarily benefit doesn't seem that  they'll get to keep any proceeds of  growth that are developed there and if  if what Jacob Reese MOG was hinting at  is what eventually materializes the  situation for people in these zones  could get far far worse if you're  essentially saying to some businesses  you can come in you won't pay any taxes  other businesses are going to have to  take the strain you're going to displace  the amount of tax burden on some other  businesses but also you're gonna you're  gonna cause real problems because if  they're not paying towards the  infrastructure in those places for the  roads and the the schools and all of the  things that sustain a place who is going  to pay for it it's probably going to be  taxpayers so you know government needs  to think really seriously about this  because they've been warned by every  senior Economist that the plans that  they're currently briefing out aren't  going to create the growth that they  need I uh just wonder though when they  were talking about growth there the one  thing you can say about this government  is we know what they stand for growth  right you can sum up in a word we can  argue about whether or not it's going to  be effective or if it's going to work or  not but we know the policy principle  that they stand for every policy is  around that what does labor stand for  look we've been pushing the government  to get a growth strategy since the  beginning so growth is that what you  stand for but but but actually Sophie I  would question this because I you know I  went into the first prime minister's  questions with listro so I think it was  absolutely clear who she stood for she  stands for the few and not the many  that's why she's loading the cost of the  energy proposals onto people and  hard-working families not onto the  office of everyone and we could have  obviously there is clearly a debate  about whether or not that is true or not  but we know that this is a government  that is ideologically we can see what it  is they believe in grow growth only  benefits everybody if everybody gains  but she has no plan to share the  proceeds of that growth she doesn't  believe in redistribution she believes  in raising the cap on Bankers bonuses  but opposing pay Rises that are in line  with inflation for working people it  just doesn't stack up and in the end  that will be the thing that undoes her  unless she changes course before Friday  and we very much hope she does I am Keen  to talk about Ukraine it's such a big  story many of our viewers can be really  concerned about what Ed Putin's been  saying he's called up 300 000 military  reservists it's the biggest mobilization  since World War one would labor be doing  anything differently if you were in  government now we make no apology for  the fact that the political parties in  Britain are united about our response to  Putin we have to stand up to Russian  aggression we have to stand very closely  with our allies in Europe and the United  States and our NATO Partners as well in  order to send a very clear and  unequivocal message to Putin and I  listened to Liz trust today it's a very  welcome change of tack from the approach  that this government has taken for the  last 12 years finally taking Russian  aggression seriously the next bit of  this jigsaw is that they've got to take  seriously the influence of the Russian  government in the United States they've  got to clean up illicit Finance in the  city of London and stop proposing  measures which they've currently got  that they're trying to get through the  statute book to make it easier for  foreign money to influence our politics  would you raise defense spending and I  think it's right to look at defense  spending at the moment you know I  haven't seen the exact details of what  they're proposing but we've always been  very supportive of the idea that you  need to have a baseline of defense  spending which you know until recently  there was a consensus around a minimum  of two percent I think that defense is  one of those things that people often  think is an add-on but look at what is  happening so would you like it to go to  three percent then I think we've got to  make sure that our troops have and our  intelligence Services have exactly what  they need in order to deal with the  threats that we Face we've got a lot of  States at the moment displaying  extremely aggressive behavior in lots of  parts of the world but to have Russia  behaving like this on the edge of our  own neighborhood means that we've got to  take this seriously okay thank you very  much for being on program

